# 11. General Examination Rules

1. Answer all questions.
2. Read command words carefully.
3. Match answer length to mark allocation.
4. Use accurate scientific terminology.
5. Provide distinct marking points.
6. Link mechanisms with consequences.
7. Quote numerical evidence when required.
8. Include correct units.
9. Show sufficient calculation working.
10. Use appropriate significant figures.
11. Distinguish correlation from causation.
12. Apply knowledge to the specific question context.
13. Use the official mark scheme for definitive grading.
14. Do not invent information not visible in diagrams.
15. For extended responses, use question-specific levels.
16. For practicals, explain variables and measurements.
17. For evaluation, justify strengths and limitations.
18. Avoid unrelated or unsupported biological explanations.

